// CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy depended on discovering an explicit "<<"
// delimiter in unconstrained generation before entering constrained SQL mode.
// Evaluation showed outputs never contained "<< >>", so the strategy likely
// stayed unconstrained throughout and never emitted a parser-validated SQL
// span. For Spider, that is fatal: we want the whole answer to be a single SQL
// query, not a free-form preamble plus a later constrained segment.
//
// This revision therefore removes delimiter search as the primary control flow.
// It immediately opens a constrained span at the start when not already inside,
// so generation is parser-guided from the first decoding step. This directly
// targets the benchmark requirement that the output be one SQL query.
//
// Inside constrained mode, the strategy is SQL-oriented but simple enough to
// verify: if the current SQL prefix is complete, close the span immediately;
// otherwise decode one constrained token. When the valid-next set is wide, we
// still bias toward schema-supplied tokens from validTokenGroups, but validity
// is always enforced by parser masking. EOS remains terminal in every branch.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
